       _u_s_e_r___d_e_s_c, if set, would prevent the descriptor from  being  considered
       empty (see mmooddiiffyy__llddtt(2)).  As a result, the only reliable way to clear
       a TLS entry is to use mmeemmsseett(3) to zero the entire _u_s_e_r___d_e_s_c structure,
       including  padding  bits,  and  then  to  set  the  _r_e_a_d___e_x_e_c___o_n_l_y  and
       _s_e_g___n_o_t___p_r_e_s_e_n_t bits.  On Linux 3.19, a _u_s_e_r___d_e_s_c  consisting  entirely
       of  zeros except for _e_n_t_r_y___n_u_m_b_e_r will also be interpreted as a request
       to clear a TLS entry, but this behaved differently on older kernels.

       Prior to Linux 3.19, the DS and ES segment registers must not reference
       TLS entries.

